1. The Core Definitions

• @Bean: Placed over a method to signal that the method instantiates, configures, and initializes an object that should be managed directly by the Spring IoC container. By default, the name of the method becomes the unique ID of the registered bean """, node=133)/>>.
• @Configuration: Placed over a class to mark it as a primary source of bean definitions. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=The%20@Bean%20annotation%20is%20used,by%20the%20Spring%20IoC%20container.), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=Annotating%20a%20class%20with%20@Configuration,a%20source%20of%20bean%20definitions.)
java
@Configuration // This class acts as our configuration center
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean // This method registers a singleton bean named "myService"
    public MyServiceImpl myService() {
        return new MyServiceImpl();
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Full Mode vs. Lite Mode (The Inter-Bean Mechanics)

The critical distinction discussed in this chapter is how Spring processes @Bean methods depending on whether or not they sit inside a @Configuration class. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=In%20common%20scenarios%2C%20@Bean%20methods,processed%20in%20a%20%22lite%22%20mode.)

A. Full Mode (Default @Configuration)

When you leave @Configuration on a class, Spring applies runtime enhancement using CGLIB proxying. This allows beans to safely call other @Bean methods in the same class to declare dependencies. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=Furthermore%2C%20@Configuration%20classes%20let%20inter%2Dbean,methods%20in%20the%20same%20class.)
• How it works: If Method A programmatically calls myService(), Spring's proxy intercepts the call. Instead of running your Java factory method a second time (which would create a duplicate object), it simply retrieves the already-created singleton instance from the container. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=cross%2Dmethod%20references%20therefore%20get%20redirected%20to%20the%20container's%20lifecycle%20management.), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=reusing%20an%20existing%20singleton%20(or,instance%20for%20the%20given%20bean.)

B. Lite Mode (Plain @Component or proxyBeanMethods = false)

If you define @Bean methods inside a regular class (like a @Component), or explicitly turn proxying off via @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false), you trigger Lite Mode. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=When%20@Bean%20methods%20are%20declared,processed%20in%20a%20%22lite%22%20mode.)
• How it works: Spring skips CGLIB subclass generation entirely, which reduces startup overhead and footprint. However, the methods lose their special container management and behave with standard Java semantics. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=As%20a%20consequence%2C%20@Bean%20methods,the%20overhead%20and%20the%20footprint.), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=When%20@Bean%20methods%20are%20declared,call%2C%20creating%20a%20new%20instance)
• The Rule: You should never call another @Bean method directly while in Lite Mode, because doing so bypasses the container and will initialize a totally separate, unmanaged object every time. Instead, you must declare dependencies as method arguments, allowing Spring to pass them in cleanly through standard autowiring. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=When%20@Bean%20methods%20are%20declared,call%2C%20creating%20a%20new%20instance), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/basic-concepts.html#:~:text=As%20a%20consequence%2C%20@Bean%20methods,a%20factory%20method%20argument%20instead.)
java
// Example of Lite Mode configuration best practice
@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false) 
public class FastAppConfig {

    @Bean
    public Engine engine() {
        return new Engine();
    }

    @Bean
    public Car car(Engine engine) { // Pass dependency as an argument instead of calling engine()!
        return new Car(engine);
    }
}


The documentation explains how to use AnnotationConfigApplicationContext, which is Spring’s core class for bootstrapping and building the application container using Java classes instead of traditional XML configuration files.
It accepts @Configuration classes, plain @Component classes, and JSR-330 annotations (@Named) as entry points to spin up the container.

1. Direct Initialization (Simple Construction)

You can directly pass your configuration class into the constructor of AnnotationConfigApplicationContext. This immediately loads and registers all the @Bean methods declared inside it. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/instantiating-container.html#:~:text=When%20@Configuration%20classes%20are%20provided,also%20registered%20as%20bean%20definitions.)
java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Spin up the container directly using the configuration class
    ApplicationContext ctx = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class); 
    
    MyService myService = ctx.getBean(MyService.class);
    myService.doStuff();
}
Use code with caution.
• Passing Component Classes Directly: You can also pass regular, un-proxied implementation classes straight into the constructor. Spring registers them as beans and automatically processes any internal @Autowired fields they contain. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/instantiating-container.html#:~:text=As%20mentioned%20earlier%2C%20AnnotationConfigApplicationContext%20is,as%20the%20following%20example%20shows), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/instantiating-container.html#:~:text=When%20@Component%20and%20JSR%2D330%20classes,within%20those%20classes%20where%20necessary.)

2. Programmatic Construction (register())

If you want to construct the container step-by-step (for instance, dynamically loading different configurations based on environment conditions), you can use the no-arg constructor combined with the register(Class<?>...) method.
• Crucial Rule: When building the container programmatically, you must explicitly invoke refresh() at the end to trigger bean instantiation and dependency injection. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/java/instantiating-container.html#:~:text=Building%20the%20Container%20Programmatically%20by,AnnotationConfigApplicationContext();%20ctx.register(AppConfig.class%2C%20OtherConfig.class);%20ctx.register(AdditionalConfig.class);%20ctx.refresh)
For programmatic setup, register() allows adding configuration classes before calling refresh(). Additionally, component scanning can be triggered via scan(String...) to automatically discover and register @Configuration and @Component classes within specified packages. For web applications, Spring provides AnnotationConfigWebApplicationContext as a web-aware alternative to XML-based context loaders.


The documentation explains how to customize the names of your beans when declaring them with the @Bean annotation, overriding Spring's default naming convention.

1. The Default Strategy

By default, when you define a bean using a Java method, Spring automatically names the bean after the method itself. [1]
java
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public Thing thing() { 
        return new Thing(); 
    }
}
Use code with caution.
• Resulting Bean Name: "thing" [1]

2. Overriding the Default Name

If your method name is long, descriptive, or doesn't match what you want to use for dependency injection points, you can pass a custom string directly into the @Bean annotation. [1]
You can override the default name by passing a value to the name attribute or using the shorthand @Bean("myThing"). [1]

3. Bean Aliasing

A single bean can be assigned multiple names or aliases by passing a String array to the name attribute. This allows the same instance to be referenced under different identifiers across application subsystems. [1]

4. Context-Wide Defaults (Spring 7.0+)

Starting with Spring 7.0, you can customize the naming strategy globally by configuring a ConfigurationBeanNameGenerator at context bootstrap or during component scanning. For example, FullyQualifiedConfigurationBeanNameGenerator assigns fully-qualified names to unnamed @Bean methods to avoid collisions. [1]

To understand how Spring's Java-based configuration works internally under the hood, let's look at the secret magic behind it. The documentation explains this by highlighting the critical difference between a class marked with @Configuration and a plain class marked with @Component.
Here is the simple, non-technical explanation of how Spring pulls off this wizardry.

1. The Big Illusion: Full Mode vs. Lite Mode

When Spring boots up and scans your classes, it splits them into two modes based on their annotations:
• Full Mode (Classes with @Configuration): Spring treats these classes as master blueprints. It intercepts your Java code to enforce its container rules (like making sure a bean is only created once).
• Lite Mode (Plain classes or @Component): Spring treats these as basic factory methods. It lets your Java code run exactly how you wrote it, with no safety nets.

2. How Full Mode Works: The CGLIB Spy (The Secret Magic)

When you write a normal Java class and call a method inside it twice, Java executes that method twice and gives you two different objects.
java
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public Engine engine() {
        return new Engine(); // "Create a new Engine"
    }

    @Bean
    public Car car() {
        // Programmatic method call inside Java config
        return new Car(engine()); 
    }
}
Use code with caution.
If this were normal Java, calling engine() inside car() would execute the engine method a second time, leaving you with two separate Engine objects.
To prevent this and protect the Singleton pattern, Spring uses a bytecode library called CGLIB.
1. Subclassing: At startup, Spring intercepts AppConfig and generates a dynamic subclass of it in memory (a "proxy" wrapper class).
2. Method Interception: When your code calls engine(), the CGLIB proxy intercepts the call. It asks the Spring container: "Hey, do we already have a bean named 'engine' registered?"
3. The Result: If it already exists, Spring completely skips executing your return new Engine(); code. Instead, it just hands back the existing singleton bean from memory!
This is why @Configuration classes and their @Bean methods cannot be marked as private or final—because CGLIB needs to be able to extend the class and override the methods at runtime.

3. How Lite Mode Works: Pure Java Semantics

If you put your @Bean methods inside a plain @Component class, or if you explicitly declare @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false), you are running in Lite Mode.
• No CGLIB Wrapper: Spring leaves your class completely alone. No proxy subclass is generated.
• Standard Java Behavior: If Method A calls Method B programmatically, it acts exactly like normal Java. It will run the code inside Method B again, creating a duplicate, unmanaged instance that Spring doesn't know about.

The Golden Rule of Lite Mode:

Because you don't have the CGLIB safety net in Lite Mode, you must never call a @Bean method directly from another method. Instead, you must pass the dependency as a method parameter so Spring can inject it safely:
java
@Component // Lite Mode!
public class AppConfigLite {

    @Bean
    public Engine engine() {
        return new Engine();
    }

    @Bean
    public Car car(Engine engine) { // <-- Pass it as an argument!
        return new Car(engine); // Perfectly safe.
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Why choose one over the other?

• Full Mode (@Configuration) is great for highly interconnected configuration files where you like programmatically linking your beans together.
• Lite Mode (proxyBeanMethods = false) is faster and has a smaller memory footprint because Spring doesn't have to generate proxy classes at runtime. (This is why modern architectures like GraalVM Native Image and Spring Boot 3+ heavily leverage Lite Mode by default).

The documentation explains how to combine and organize multiple Java configuration classes, allowing you to modularize your setup instead of stuffing all bean definitions into one giant file.
Here is the simple, non-technical explanation, followed by real-world scenario questions to test your understanding.

The Easy Explanation 🧩

Imagine your application configuration is like building a house. You don't want the plumbing blueprint, electrical layout, and interior design drawn on the exact same piece of paper. You want separate sheets that stitch together.
Spring provides three main ways to compose and connect these files:

1. The Modular Loader: @Import

Instead of forcing your main() method to load every configuration class explicitly, you can use @Import to link them together.
• If ConfigB has @Import(ConfigA.class), loading only ConfigB will automatically pull in all the beans defined in ConfigA as well.

2. Cross-File Wiring: Parameter vs. Field Injection

When configuration files split, beans in one file often depend on beans in another. Spring allows you to connect them across files:
• Parameter Injection (Recommended): You list the external bean as a parameter in your @Bean method. Spring cleanly resolves it.
• Field/Autowired Injection: You can @Autowired the configuration classes themselves or fields. Warning: Doing this can sometimes cause beans to initialize too early, so parameter injection is preferred.

3. The Bridge: @ImportResource (Java + XML)

If you are working on a legacy app that still uses old Spring XML files, you don't have to rewrite everything. You can place @ImportResource("classpath:old-config.xml") on your Java @Configuration class to instantly load the XML definitions into your Java context.

Scenario-Based Questions (Interview Style)


Question 1: The Broken Cross-File Dependency

Scenario: You split your application into DatabaseConfig (which creates a DataSource bean) and SecurityConfig (which creates a UserDetailsService bean). UserDetailsService requires the DataSource to look up users. You run the app by initializing:
java
