Here is a comprehensive set of scenario-based questions covering the entire Java-based Container Configuration module we have explored. These questions simulate real-world debugging, architectural choices, and performance trade-offs.

Tier 1: @Bean, @Configuration, Full vs. Lite Mode


Question 1: The Accidental Duplicate Bean Bug

Scenario: You are working in a performance-critical application. A developer tries to optimize startup time by declaring proxyBeanMethods = false to run the configuration in Lite Mode. They write the following code:
java
@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public Engine engine() {
        return new Engine();
    }

    @Bean
    public Car sedan() {
        return new Car(engine()); 
    }

    @Bean
    public Car suv() {
        return new Car(engine());
    }
}
Use code with caution.
During a heap dump analysis, you notice that there are three instances of Engine in memory instead of one.
• Why did this happen, and how do you fix this code while keeping the configuration in Lite Mode?
See Answer
Because proxyBeanMethods = false turns off CGLIB proxying, calling engine() acts like plain Java. Every call executes new Engine() again.

Question 2: The private Configuration Crash

Scenario: A developer wants to restrict visibility of a configuration class so it cannot be instantiated or subclassed outside its package. They write the following code:
java
@Configuration
public final class InternalConfig {

    @Bean
    private EncryptionService encryptionService() {
        // ...
    }
}
Use code with caution.
When booting the application, Spring immediately crashes with an initialization exception before it even runs your code.
• What internal mechanism caused this crash, and what are the specific rules regarding Java modifiers for Full Mode configurations?
See Answer
In Full Mode, Spring uses CGLIB proxying to create a dynamic subclass of your configuration class in memory.
Therefore, Full Mode configuration classes and non-static factory methods must be overridable (not private or final).

Tier 2: AnnotationConfigApplicationContext & Bootstrapping


Question 3: The Ghost Bean Mystery

Scenario: You are programmatically building a diagnostic tool using a custom main method. You initialize the container, register your configurations step-by-step, and attempt to fetch a bean:
java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    AnnotationConfigApplicationContext ctx = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext();
    ctx.register(AppConfig.class);
    
    MyService service = ctx.getBean(MyService.class); // Exception thrown here!
}
Use code with caution.
The application crashes with an IllegalStateException or NoSuchBeanDefinitionException.
• What critical step did the developer forget when building the container programmatically?
See Answer
When using the no-arg constructor and programmatically calling register(), the container does not automatically boot up. You must explicitly call ctx.refresh(); to instruct Spring to process the registered definitions, instantiate singletons, and perform dependency injection.

Question 4: The Interface Visibility Trap

Scenario: You define a bean using its interface as the return type to encourage loose coupling:
java
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public PaymentService paymentService() {
        return new StripePaymentServiceImpl();
    }
}
Use code with caution.
In another component, a developer tries to autowire the concrete implementation directly: @Autowired private StripePaymentServiceImpl stripeService;. Depending on when this component is initialized relative to the configuration order, this autowiring randomly fails.
• Why is this type-matching unreliable, and what is the best practice recommended by the documentation?
See Answer
Spring's advanced type prediction is limited to the declared return type (PaymentService). The container only learns about the concrete implementation type (StripePaymentServiceImpl) after the singleton bean is fully instantiated. If another component evaluates its injection points before this bean is constructed, type matching fails.

Tier 3: Composing Configurations & Lifecycle


Question 5: The Static Factory Post-Processor Deadlock

Scenario: You want to register a PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer bean to strictly validate your properties. You place it inside your main configuration class:
java
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Autowired
    private Environment env;

    @Bean
    public PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer configurer() {
        return new PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer();
    }
}
Use code with caution.
When starting the app, you notice that @Autowired fields on AppConfig are completely ignored and resolve to null.
• Why did the presence of this specific bean break dependency injection on its own configuration class, and how do you fix it?
See Answer
PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer implements BeanFactoryPostProcessor. These infrastructural beans must be initialized very early in the Spring lifecycle (before normal beans are processed). Because it was declared as a regular instance method, Spring had to force-initialize the entire AppConfig instance prematurely, bypassing the AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor.

Question 6: The Database Server Shutdown Crash

Scenario: You are migrating a legacy Jakarta EE application to Spring's Java configuration. You fetch a managed database resource using a programmatic JNDI lookup:
java
@Bean
public DataSource dataSource() throws NamingException {
    return (DataSource) new JndiTemplate().lookup("java:comp/env/jdbc/myDS");
}
Use code with caution.
When you stop your application, the application server logs a severe error: DataSource connection pool closed unexpectedly while connections were active.
• What automatic Spring feature caused this error, and how do you disable it for external resources?
See Answer
By default, Spring's Java configuration detects if a bean has a public close() or shutdown() method and automatically enlists it in a destruction callback (inferred mode). For resources managed outside the application (like a JNDI DataSource managed by the app server), this causes a double-destruction bug.

Tier 4: Spring 6.2+ & Advanced Capabilities


Question 7: The Background Thread Blocking Trap (Spring 6.2+)

Scenario: To optimize a slow microservice startup, you take advantage of Spring 6.2's asynchronous bootstrap feature. You have a HeavyReportGenerator bean that takes 5 seconds to load, and a ReportController that relies on it:
java
@Bean(bootstrap = BACKGROUND)
public HeavyReportGenerator reportGenerator() {
    return new HeavyReportGenerator(); // 5-second computation
}

@Bean
public ReportController reportController(HeavyReportGenerator rg) {
    return new ReportController(rg);
}
Use code with caution.
Even after setting this up, your main bootstrap thread still completely freezes for 5 seconds when constructing ReportController.
• Why did the main thread block, and what architectural pattern must you use in the dependent bean to keep initialization truly concurrent?
See Answer
The main bootstrap thread blocks because ReportController declares a standard, non-lazy injection point for HeavyReportGenerator. Spring is forced to stall the main thread until the background thread finishes building that dependency.

Question 8: The Dynamic Scope Proxy Leak

Scenario: You declare a web-scoped object inside a singleton bean:
java
@Bean
@SessionScope
public UserPreferences userPreferences() {
    return new UserPreferences();
}

@Bean
public DashboardService dashboardService() {
    DashboardService service = new DashboardService();
    service.setPreferences(userPreferences()); // Injected into a singleton
    return service;
}
Use code with caution.
DashboardService is a singleton (created once at startup). UserPreferences changes for every user session.
• How does Spring ensure that DashboardService accesses the correct user's preferences for each request, rather than getting stuck with the data of whoever logged in first?
See Answer
Under the hood, Spring creates a Scoped Proxy (via proxyMode). When userPreferences() is called inside dashboardService(), Spring does not inject a raw data object. Instead, it injects a smart, lightweight proxy object. Every time a method is called on that proxy, it dynamically forwards the request to the current HTTP session context to look up the correct user data.



The documentation explains how to take an XML-centric approach to managing your application container while still taking advantage of modern Java-based @Configuration classes.
This is an essential design pattern when you are working on a large, legacy application that has thousands of lines of existing Spring XML configuration. Instead of doing a massive, risky rewrite of your entire codebase into Java configuration, you leave the XML as the primary driver ("the boss") and introduce your new Java @Configuration classes incrementally.
Spring provides two primary methods to accomplish this:

Method 1: Declaring @Configuration Classes as Plain <bean/> Elements

Because a class annotated with @Configuration is ultimately just another bean in the eyes of Spring, you can register it inside your XML file using standard <bean> syntax.

1. The Java Code

You write a standard configuration class using annotations for internal wiring:
You can implement this by writing a Java configuration class (such as com.acme.AppConfig) that utilizes annotations like @Autowired and @Bean.

2. The Primary XML File (system-test-config.xml)

In your XML configuration, enable annotation processing via <context:annotation-config/> and register your Java configuration class as a standard bean alongside your infrastructure beans.

3. How You Boot It

Start the application using an XML-based context reader like ClassPathXmlApplicationContext. Spring will parse the XML, process the annotations, and register the Java-defined @Bean methods alongside your XML beans.

Method 2: Using <context:component-scan/> to Autodetect @Configuration Classes

To avoid manually declaring every Java configuration class in XML, you can leverage classpath component scanning. Because @Configuration is meta-annotated with @Component, replacing manual bean entries with <context:component-scan base-package="com.acme"/> implicitly enables annotation processing and automatically discovers and parses your Java configuration classes and inner @Bean methods.

Summary Checklist: Which Direction are you facing?


Strategy	Who is "The Boss"?	Entrypoint Annotation / Class	Use Case
XML-Centric	XML Files	ClassPathXmlApplicationContext	Legacy migration; adding modern Java blocks to a large, pre-existing XML ecosystem.
Java-Centric	Java Classes	@ImportResource / AnnotationConfigApplicationContext	Modern applications that only need XML for tricky namespaces (like legacy security or integration configurations).

The documentation explains the exact opposite of the previous approach: a Java-centric architecture where a @Configuration class is the absolute master blueprint ("the boss"), but it pulls in external XML files using the @ImportResource annotation.
This pattern is highly common when you are writing a modern, greenfield Spring application in Java, but you need to use a specific framework or legacy plugin that only provides configuration instructions in an XML format (such as complex Spring Security filters, legacy Enterprise Integration patterns, or third-party vendor configurations).

How It Works: The Master Blueprint

Instead of using an XML file to boot the system, you use a standard Java entry point and instruct it to absorb the XML layout cleanly:

1. The Legacy XML File (classpath:properties-config.xml)

Imagine you have a standalone XML file that configures a database repository or data beans:
xml
<beans xmlns="http://springframework.org"
       xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://springframework.org 
                           https://springframework.org">

    <!-- A bean defined cleanly in XML -->
    <bean id="accountRepository" class="com.acme.repository.JdbcAccountRepository"/>
</beans>
Use code with caution.

2. The Master Java Configuration (AppConfig.class)

You write your standard configuration class and point to the XML path using @ImportResource. This tells Spring: "Build all my Java beans, but go read this XML file and register everything inside it into the exact same container."
java
@Configuration
@ImportResource("classpath:properties-config.xml") // <-- Pulls the XML into Java
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public TransferService transferService(AccountRepository accountRepository) {
        // The accountRepository bean defined in XML is injected right here!
        return new TransferServiceImpl(accountRepository);
    }
}
Use code with caution.

3. How You Boot It

You initialize the entire container using Java-centric context loaders, totally bypassing XML-based file readers:
java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Completely Java-centric startup
    ApplicationContext ctx = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);
    TransferService service = ctx.getBean(TransferService.class);
}
Use code with caution.

The Big Advantage: Bidirectional Wiring

When you use @ImportResource, Spring merges the XML definitions and Java definitions into a single, unified pool of beans. This means wiring works both ways perfectly:
• Java can inject XML beans: As shown in the code above, the Java method parameter AccountRepository seamlessly picks up the bean defined inside the <bean id="accountRepository"> tag.
• XML can inject Java beans: If an XML bean definition uses a <property name="myService" ref="transferService"/>, it will successfully discover and wire the transferService bean defined via Java's @Bean.

Scenario-Based Interview Question

Scenario: You are migrating an old application. You create a master AppConfig class and add @ImportResource("classpath:legacy-context.xml"). Inside legacy-context.xml, there is a bean defined as <bean id="customerService" class="com.acme.CustomerService"/>.
In your AppConfig class, a developer wants to use that customer service bean, so they write:
java
@Bean
public DashboardController dashboardController() {
    return new DashboardController(customerService()); // Compilation Error!
}
Use code with caution.
The compiler throws a major error because customerService() is a regular Java method that doesn't exist inside the AppConfig class code.
